{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 2, :comments ["<b>And I will make you a great nation.</b> Because travel causes three things — it diminishes fruitfulness and increase, and it diminishes wealth, and it diminishes renown — therefore he needed these three blessings: He promised him concerning children, concerning wealth, and concerning renown." "<b>And I will bless you.</b> With wealth (Bereshit Rabbah)." "<b>And you shall be a blessing.</b> The blessings are placed in your hand; until now they were in My hand — I blessed Adam and Noach, and from now on you shall bless whomever you wish (Bereshit Rabbah). Another interpretation: <b>And I will make you a great nation</b> — this is what they say, 'Elohei Avraham'; <b>and I will bless you</b> — this is what they say, 'Elohei Yitzchak'; <b>and I will make your name great</b> — this is what they say, 'Elohei Yaakov.' One might think they seal with all of them — Scripture teaches: ״וֶהְיֵה בְרָכָה״ — with you they seal, and not with them." "<b>From your land.</b> But had he not already left there with his father and come as far as Haran? Rather, thus He said to him: Distance yourself further from there, and leave your father's house." "<b>Which I will show you.</b> He did not reveal the land to him immediately, so as to make it beloved in his eyes, and to give him reward for every single utterance. So too: ״אֶת בִּנְךָ אֶת יְחִידְךָ אֲשֶׁר אָהַבְתָּ אֶת יִצְחָק״ (Genesis 22); so too: ״עַל אַחַד הֶהָרִים אֲשֶׁר אֹמַר אֵלֶיךָ״, and similarly: ״וּקְרָא אֵלֶיהָ אֶת הַקְּרִיאָה אֲשֶׁר אָנֹכִי דֹבֵר אֵלֶיךָ״ (Jonah 3)."], :book :genesis, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 12, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:58:26.395928932Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}